<h1>Battery materials &amp; manufacturing</h1>
<p>The inputs, the process and the evidence: how material choice becomes a battery, and what a factory record should prove.</p>
<h2>How material choice becomes performance</h2>
<p>The cathode sets the energy and the cost; the anode sets the charge rate and the cycle life; the electrolyte and separator set the safety window. A change in one material rarely improves every metric at once, which is why a supplier's "new formula" claim should be read as a trade-off, not a free gain. The articles below follow the material from incoming inspection to the formed cell.</p>
<h2>What a factory QC record should prove</h2>
<ul>
<li>Incoming material identity and lot, with a COA that matches the material actually consumed.</li>
<li>Coating weight and thickness within the stated tolerance, measured on the production line.</li>
<li>Calendering density and electrode dimensions, because both change the cell's power and energy balance.</li>
<li>Formation and aging data, including the cells that failed and how they were dispositioned.</li>
<li>End-of-line capacity, resistance and self-discharge, traceable to the cell serial number.</li>
</ul>
<p>A record that lists only the final pass rate cannot explain a field failure. The value is in the intermediate measurements and the traceability between them.</p>
<h2>Where to go next</h2>
<p>Use the lead-acid or lithium technology cluster for the component-level explanation, the testing site for the standards that verify a claim, and the market site for the material price context behind a quotation.</p>

